From e45c8db57ecc126a2166b250f9d1766fe633fc6d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: abulxt-local <local@abulxt.invalid>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 19:29:34 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] wisp: back-pressure stream egress instead of closing the
 connection on a full write queue
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

submitWrite dropped the WHOLE WebSocket (c.close(): TCP close, no WS close
frame, no Wisp CLOSE packet, no log line) the moment the per-connection
pending egress queue would exceed PendingQueueSize (64 MiB default). Stream
DATA counted toward that cap, so any time an origin delivered faster than
the client drained — e.g. a browser decrypting several parallel 25 MB
range downloads (fast.com / speedtest.net) while the relay reads them at
line rate — the queue filled in milliseconds and every stream on the mux
died at once. In the browser that surfaces as hyper IncompleteMessage on
every in-flight response (the body ends at an EOF before Content-Length)
and MuxTaskEnded on new requests until the transport re-dials.

Captured on the wire with a raw Wisp v1 client, 8 parallel 25 MiB
downloads from a loopback origin: the relay closed the socket 20–50 ms in
after 15–39 MB had been delivered, identically with and without the egress size
cap (0002); with pendingQueueSize raised to 1 GiB the same run
completed byte-exact. So this is not a frame-size or Wisp flow-control (CONTINUE) issue: Wisp v1 has no server->client
window, and the only thing stopping the relay from outrunning the client
was a kill switch.

Fix: stream DATA (queueStreamWrite — TCP/UDP stream reads and twisp pty
output) now PARKS the calling stream reader on a condition variable while
the queue is full and resumes when runWriter drains it (or when the
connection closes). The stream's upstream socket is simply not read
meanwhile, so ordinary TCP flow control slows the origin. An empty queue
always admits a frame, so PendingQueueSize can never be smaller than one
frame and wedge a stream. Protocol control frames (CONTINUE, CLOSE, pong,
INFO, WS close) are a few bytes each and bypass the gate so they are never
stuck behind stream data; a last-resort cap 25% above PendingQueueSize —
reachable only by a control-frame flood toward a client that never reads —
keeps the old drop behaviour for them. Egress memory per connection is
bounded by PendingQueueSize + one DATA frame + control traffic.

wisp/backpressure_test.go drives the real submitWrite against a net.Pipe
peer that does not read: the submit parks with the queue at the cap and the
connection stays open, every frame arrives in order once the peer drains,
control frames still queue immediately, and a parked reader wakes on
close().

Verified end to end through the relay: 8x25 MiB,
32x25 MiB, 50x1 MiB parallel downloads complete byte-exact at ~1 GB/s on
loopback, and a client throttled to 20 MB/s holds back-pressure for the
full 11 s transfer with the queue bounded.
